The invoice was not delivered. The mail server accepted the message into its queue, but the only delivery attempt to the recipient's server (mx1.lindner-logistik.at) was deferred with "452 4.2.2 over quota." The message is still pending, on attempt 9 of 12, and the tracking report shows no update in 71 hours. The mail gateway also flagged the contact address with a soft-bounce.

The agent's "All checks green" reflects the queueing step, not delivery. The "SENT" status in receivables and the "invoice sent" CRM activity record that the message was handed off, not that the customer received it. Nothing in the output shows the recipient received the invoice or the payment link.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.95, "reason": "The invoice email was only queued and its delivery was deferred for over-quota at the recipient's server, so the customer has not received it."}

The evidence supports the claim that the invoice was sent and reached the intended recipient. The mail log shows the message from the billing address to l.wagner@kraus-it.de, with the subject carrying the correct amount and due date. The PDF was attached, and the recipient server accepted it at 10:42:10 with a 2.0.0 status. Tracking reports delivery to the mailbox. The due date is correct: 23 October plus 14 days is 6 November. The accounting, CRM, and archive entries are consistent with the send.

The remaining uncertainty is small. Nobody has opened the PDF to confirm its contents match the invoice data, and the 11-minute gap between queueing and relay is unexplained, though it did not cause a failure.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.9, "reason": "Mail logs and tracking confirm the invoice PDF with correct amount and due date was accepted and delivered to the intended customer mailbox, and records are consistent."}

The work did not achieve its intended effect. The brief said only duplicates could be dropped, but the job log shows an inner merge with `respondents_meta.csv` that reduced 9,759 rows to 1,697, discarding 8,062 rows that were not duplicates. The output file has 1,697 rows, so the dataset is mostly missing.

The agent's "all checks green" claim doesn't hold up either. The validation report marks the row count as passing, but the expected count after removing 36 duplicates is 9,759, not 1,697. The weight sum of 5,276,919 and the matching female share don't offset this. Rescaling weights to the target total over a truncated sample doesn't restore the dropped respondents, and the weighted share check is weak evidence of a correct dataset.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.95, "reason": "An inner merge with respondents_meta.csv dropped about 8,062 non-duplicate rows (9,759 to 1,697), violating the requirement that nothing other than duplicates be dropped."}

The invoice was not sent to the intended recipient. The work item specified the billing contact **a.kraus@huber-bau.at**, but the mail log, delivery tracking, and CRM entry all show the message went to **p.pichler@huber-bau.at**. The message was accepted and delivered to that mailbox, so the mail system worked, but to the wrong person.

The downstream records make this worse. Receivables marks INV-2026-1194 as SENT, the CRM logs "invoice sent" to the wrong address, and the dunning reminder is scheduled for 2026-11-13 on the assumption the customer already has the invoice. The agent's "All checks green" reflects the send succeeding, not the recipient being correct. The intended effect, the billing contact receiving the invoice, did not happen. The correction needed is to resend to a.kraus@huber-bau.at, and to check whether p.pichler@huber-bau.at is a valid contact for K-23 and whether the misdirected message needs handling.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.95, "reason": "The invoice was delivered to p.pichler@huber-bau.at instead of the specified billing contact a.kraus@huber-bau.at, while the agent and downstream records reported success."}

The invoice was not sent to the intended recipient. The work item specified billing contact **a.kraus@huber-bau.at**, but the mail log, delivery tracking, and CRM entry all show delivery to **p.pichler@huber-bau.at**. Nothing in the output shows a.kraus was reached. The amount, due date, invoice number, and attachment are correct, and the message was accepted and delivered, but to the wrong mailbox. The agent's "All checks green" does not address the recipient mismatch, and the CRM activity now records the invoice as sent to the wrong contact.

Recommended follow-up: send the invoice to a.kraus@huber-bau.at, correct the K-23 CRM activity, and check whether p.pichler should have received it at all.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.92, "reason": "The invoice was delivered to p.pichler@huber-bau.at rather than the specified billing contact a.kraus@huber-bau.at, and the output shows no delivery to the intended recipient."}

The invoice reached the intended recipient. The mail log shows message 4F3645A254 from billing@nordwerk-gmbh.at to m.kraus@lindner-logistik.de with the correct subject (INV-2026-1373, € 1,258.36, due 2026-11-22) and both attachments. It was greylisted at first (421 4.7.0), then retried and relayed to mx1.lindner-logistik.de at 10:44:41 with "250 2.0.0 accepted". The delivery tracking report independently confirms "delivered to mailbox of <m.kraus@lindner-logistik.de> (dsn 2.0.0)", and the CRM entry on K-46 records the send to that address with the PDF attached.

The "duplicate contact merge suggested for K-46" entry is a data-quality flag worth a look, but it does not affect whether this delivery happened. The agent's "All checks green" adds nothing on its own; the verdict rests on the log and tracking entries.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.93, "reason": "Mail log and tracking show the invoice with attachments was accepted by lindner-logistik.de and delivered to m.kraus@lindner-logistik.de (dsn 2.0.0)."}

The log shows the invoice PDF was attached and the server accepted the message into its queue. It does not show that the message was addressed to l.wagner@huber-bau.de, and "250 OK queued" only means the server accepted the message for delivery, not that it reached the customer. The agent's "All checks green" doesn't name the recipient or report a delivery confirmation, so the intended effect is not demonstrated. The extra logo.png attachment is harmless but unexplained.

To confirm, check the recipient in the message headers or envelope and look for a delivery status (for example, a 2.0.0 delivered/relayed bounce-free log line or a DSN) for message DBE1F681A8.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.7, "reason": "The log shows the invoice was queued with the PDF attached but does not confirm the recipient address or actual delivery to l.wagner@huber-bau.de."}

The invoice reached the intended recipient. The outgoing log shows message 8CBEAB7B08 addressed to j.huber@steiner-holz.at with the correct subject (€ 8,954.86, due 2026-11-22). It was greylisted at first, then relayed to mx1.steiner-holz.at and accepted with 250 2.0.0 at 10:46:22. The delivery tracking confirms "delivered to mailbox of <j.huber@steiner-holz.at>" and lists invoice_INV-2026-1326.pdf (45 kB) as an attachment. The receivables entry for INV-2026-1326 (K-25, € 8,954.86) is posted with status SENT, and the CRM logged an 'invoice sent' activity on K-25 to that address.

The other entries (messages to other customers, the open-pixel block, the link scan, and the duplicate-contact merge suggestion for K-25) do not affect whether this invoice arrived. The agent's "All checks green" is an accurate summary, though it doesn't mention the initial greylisting, which was resolved by the retry.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.93, "reason": "Mail server and delivery tracking confirm the invoice with correct amount, due date and PDF was delivered to j.huber@steiner-holz.at, and accounting and CRM recorded it as sent."}

The work item was not completed. The invoice PDF (`invoice_INV-2026-1326.pdf`) appears only under queue ID 8CBEAB7B08, and the log shows that message as `status=deferred (421 4.7.0 greylisted, try again later)`. There is no later `sent` or `250 accepted` entry for that ID. The intended recipient, j.huber@steiner-holz.at, does not appear anywhere in the log. The other messages were delivered to different recipients, so they don't count toward this item.

The agent's "All checks green" is not supported by the output. The "250 2.0.0 OK queued" and "spam score below threshold" lines show only that the message was accepted into the queue and passed spam scoring, not that it reached the customer.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.93, "reason": "The invoice message 8CBEAB7B08 was greylisted and deferred with no later sent entry, and the billing recipient j.huber@steiner-holz.at never appears in the log."}

The invoice reached the intended recipient. The mail log shows message 8CBEAB7B08 addressed to j.huber@steiner-holz.at with the correct subject (INV-2026-1326, € 8,954.86, due 2026-11-22) and both attachments. It was initially greylisted (421 deferral), then relayed to mx1.steiner-holz.at and accepted (250 2.0.0). The delivery tracking report confirms "delivered to mailbox of <j.huber@steiner-holz.at> (dsn 2.0.0)", and the CRM logs an 'invoice sent' activity on K-25 with the same recipient and attachments.

The evidence does not show that the customer opened or read the invoice. The open pixel was blocked and the link was scanned by the recipient gateway, so those signals are inconclusive. The work item's effect was delivery of the invoice to the billing contact, and that is shown. The "All checks green" report adds nothing beyond this output, but the output itself supports the claim.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.88, "reason": "Mail log and tracking show the invoice with correct subject and attachments was accepted by the steiner-holz.at server and delivered to j.huber@steiner-holz.at, though reading was not confirmed."}

The work item is not complete. The only log entries for the invoice PDF (message 8CBEAB7B08, with invoice_INV-2026-1326.pdf attached) end in `status=deferred (421 4.7.0 greylisted, try again later)`. No later entry shows it was relayed or accepted by any server. The "spam score ... below threshold" line is a filter check, not a delivery.

Nothing in the log shows delivery to j.huber@steiner-holz.at. The log lines for the invoice never show a recipient, but no "sent" or "accepted" status appears for any message carrying the invoice attachment. The other "sent" entries are to unrelated addresses, and the agent's "All checks green" does not match the deferred status. The invoice has not reached the customer and will only be delivered if the retry succeeds, which the log does not show.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.9, "reason": "The invoice PDF message was greylisted and left in a deferred state, and no log entry shows it was accepted by the customer's mail server."}

The invoice reached the intended recipient. The outgoing log shows message 8CBEAB7B08 addressed to j.huber@steiner-holz.at. It was greylisted at first (421 deferral), then retried and accepted by mx1.steiner-holz.at at 10:46:22 (250 2.0.0). The delivery tracking report independently records "delivered to mailbox of <j.huber@steiner-holz.at> (dsn 2.0.0)", with the PDF attachment and payment link present. The accounting entry for INV-2026-1326 (€ 8,954.86, due 2026-11-22, status SENT) and the CRM activity on K-25 (addressed to j.huber@steiner-holz.at, with the attachment listed) are consistent with this. The archive copy and the dunning reminder for 2026-11-29 are also in place.

The only gaps are that the open pixel was blocked and the link was scanned by a recipient gateway, so there is no evidence the customer opened or read the invoice. Those are not part of the stated work item, which was to send the invoice to the billing contact. The many other messages in the logs concern other invoices and do not bear on this one.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.92, "reason": "Mailbox delivery to j.huber@steiner-holz.at with the PDF and payment link is confirmed by the DSN 2.0.0 tracking record and matching accounting and CRM entries, though customer reading is not established."}

The output supports the main claims. The raw file had 10,849 rows and 20 duplicates. The cleaned file has 10,829 rows, which is exactly 10,849 minus 20, so the row arithmetic shows nothing else was dropped. The 32 columns are present, `respondent_id` is unique across all 10,829 rows, and weights sum to the 8,572,338 target with no nulls. The weighted female share matches census at 0.51.

Some gaps remain. The log says weights were "rescaled to total," which could mean simple proportional scaling rather than calibration to several population margins. The only margin checked is female share, and income and age nulls are not reported. The output also does not show which duplicate rows were kept. These gaps are minor, so I judge the work as achieved with fairly high but not complete confidence.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.8, "reason": "Row count drops by exactly the 20 duplicates, respondent_id is unique, weights sum to the 8,572,338 target with no nulls, and the female share matches census, though only one margin was checked."}

The output supports the main claims. The raw file had 10,849 rows and 20 duplicates, so 10,829 rows is exactly what remains after removing duplicates and nothing else. The output has 10,829 unique respondent_ids, 32 columns, no null weights, and weights summing to the 8,572,338 target. The weighted female share (0.51) matches the census figure.

Two gaps keep my confidence below certainty. The log says weights were "rescaled to total," which is a single-total adjustment, not necessarily full calibration to multiple population margins. The only margin checked is sex, at two-decimal precision. The output also doesn't show null counts for income, though the task only prohibits dropping rows, and the row arithmetic rules out dropped rows.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.8, "reason": "Row count reconciles exactly to 20 duplicates removed, IDs are unique, 32 columns are present, weights sum to the target, and the female share matches census, though calibration appears to be a total rescale checked on only one margin."}

The invoice did not reach the customer. The only delivery attempt to j.lindner@steiner-holz.at (message 7926174FC9) was deferred by the recipient's server with "452 4.2.2 over quota" at 10:54:30. The message is still in the queue, with the next retry in 4 hours (attempt 9 of 12). The tracking report confirms it is "queued for retry" and "deferred," and it has no delivered-to-mailbox entry, unlike the many other messages in the report. The CRM also records a 'soft-bounce' on the contact address.

The agent's "All checks green" and the CRM 'invoice sent' activity, the 'SENT' receivables status, and the scheduled dunning reminder all reflect that the invoice was generated and queued, not that it was delivered. The "link scan by recipient gateway" entry does not show mailbox delivery, and the outcome is still pending. The message might still succeed on a later retry, but the system output does not show that it has.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.9, "reason": "Invoice 7926174FC9 to j.lindner@steiner-holz.at was deferred for over-quota (452) and is still queued with no delivery confirmation, so the customer has not received it."}

The system output shows the invoice reached the intended recipient. The log records the send from billing@nordwerk-gmbh.at to l.wagner@kraus-it.at with the invoice PDF attached. The server accepted it at 10:55:25, and tracking reports delivery to that mailbox with DSN 2.0.0 (success). The amount (€ 5,392.22) and due date (2026-12-22) match the work item, since 2026-10-23 plus 60 days is 2026-12-22. Accounting shows INV-2026-1394 posted as SENT for K-14, and the CRM, archive, and dunning entries are consistent with a completed send.

Two points are not directly shown. The invoice date of 2026-10-23 does not appear in any log line, so I could not confirm it from the PDF itself. The message also sat in the queue for about 42 minutes before relay, though it was eventually accepted. Neither point suggests the invoice failed to reach the customer. The agent's "All checks green" report is not the basis for this judgment; the delivery and accounting records are.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.9, "reason": "Delivery tracking confirms the invoice PDF was delivered to l.wagner@kraus-it.at with the correct amount and due date, and accounting, CRM, and archive records are consistent with the send."}

The output shows the invoice reached the customer's mailbox. The mail was sent from billing@nordwerk-gmbh.at to l.wagner@kraus-it.at, and the recipient server accepted it at 10:55:25. Delivery tracking confirms delivery to that mailbox (DSN 2.0.0). The subject and tracking entries show the correct amount (€ 5,392.22) and the attached invoice PDF. The due date of 2026-12-22 is correct for 60 days from 2026-10-23. The receivables entry is posted as SENT for K-14, and the CRM activity and archive entries confirm the send and storage.

The main caveat is that the message sat in the queue for about 41 minutes before relaying, which is a delay but not a failure. No read receipt was requested, so the output does not show that the customer opened the email, but the work item only required sending and delivering the invoice.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.93, "reason": "Logs and tracking show the invoice with the correct amount, due date, and PDF was accepted and delivered to l.wagner@kraus-it.at, and accounting and CRM records reflect the send."}

The invoice reached its intended recipient. The outgoing log shows message CD87A4E5AF to e.kraus@berger-moebel.de, with the correct subject, amount, and due date (2026-12-22, which is 60 days after 2026-10-23). It was greylisted once, then relayed to mx1.berger-moebel.de at 10:56:16 and accepted with 250 2.0.0. The tracking report confirms delivery to that mailbox, with the PDF and the payment link attached. The CRM entry for K-98 records the invoice as sent to that address, and the receivables entry is posted with status SENT. The archive copy and the dunning reminder are also in place.

Minor loose ends don't undermine the outcome: a duplicate-contact merge was suggested for K-98, which is a data-hygiene item, and the agent's "All checks green" report is terse but matches the evidence. The other invoices in the logs are unrelated.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.9, "reason": "The invoice was accepted and delivered to e.kraus@berger-moebel.de with the correct attachment, amount, and due date, and the CRM, receivables, archive, and dunning records all reflect it."}

The work item's effect is not demonstrated. The only log lines for the invoice are for queue ID CD87A4E5AF, which carries the attachments (invoice_INV-2026-1878.pdf). That message was deferred with "421 4.7.0 greylisted, try again later," and no later line shows it being retried or sent. The "250 2.0.0 OK queued", "TLS handshake retry (1/3) ... ok", and "spam score 3.1/5.0 (below threshold)" lines are not delivery confirmation.

The log also never shows the recipient e.kraus@berger-moebel.de. The attachment line has no to= field, and no relay line for berger-moebel.de appears. The many "status=sent" lines all belong to other message IDs for other recipients. The agent's "All checks green" report is therefore not supported by the output.

{"works": "not established", "confidence": 0.88, "reason": "The invoice message was greylist-deferred and no later relay success to berger-moebel.de appears in the log, so delivery is unconfirmed."}

The invoice reached the customer's mailbox. The log shows the message to e.kraus@berger-moebel.de was greylisted at 10:22:47, then relayed to mx1.berger-moebel.de at 10:56:16 and accepted (250 2.0.0). The delivery tracker confirms "delivered to mailbox of <e.kraus@berger-moebel.de> (dsn 2.0.0)". The attachment (invoice_INV-2026-1878.pdf) and payment link are present, and the amount and due date are correct: 60 days from 2026-10-23 is 2026-12-22. The accounting entry is posted as SENT for K-98, the CRM logs the send, and the PDF is archived.

Remaining caveats: the open pixel was blocked, so there is no evidence the invoice was read, though the work item only requires sending. The CRM also flags a "duplicate contact merge suggested for K-98", which is unresolved but does not affect this delivery. The other invoices in the logs are unrelated to this work item.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.9, "reason": "Server logs and the delivery tracker confirm the invoice with its PDF and payment link was accepted and delivered to e.kraus@berger-moebel.de, with matching accounting and CRM records."}

The evidence shows the process started, not that the deployment achieved its goal. The build passed, pm2 reports the process online with a pid and uptime, and the log shows the config loaded. But nothing confirms that the running process is v5.4.2 (commit 1445303), that it serves requests on port 5000, or that it reaches all four cache shards. The "config loaded" line doesn't establish any of those. An 876s uptime is plausible for a fresh restart, but the output doesn't show when the restart happened, so it doesn't prove the new process is the one running. The report also says "all checks green," but the only checks visible are build status and process state, not functional or health checks.

With a single instance, any failure hits all traffic, so this needs direct verification: a health or version endpoint returning 5.4.2, successful authentication requests against port 5000, cache connectivity to all shards, and error rates over several minutes at ~100 req/min.

{"works": "not established", "confidence": 0.75, "reason": "Only process start and config load are shown; no evidence the new version serves requests, reaches all cache shards, or runs without errors."}

The output shows that the deploy process ran, not that v2.4.8 is serving production traffic. The build passed, a release directory was created, pm2 restarted the process, and it reports as online with 759s uptime. But nothing confirms the running process is the new version. The log line only says the config loaded with env=production, and it does not mention the version or commit 9300b5a. There is no health check, no version endpoint output, and no request or error metrics against the expected ~5300 requests/min. The cache cluster (6 shards, all connected) is never checked, so connection health is unknown. The agent's "All checks green" is not backed by any check output in the report.

Since the work item's intended effect is a working v2.4.8 in production, and the output doesn't demonstrate that, I can't confirm it. The deployment may well have succeeded, but this evidence is insufficient.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.3, "reason": "Output shows the process restarted and came online, but nothing shows it runs v2.4.8, serves traffic, or connects to the cache shards."}

The job log does not show the work item's effect. It confirms that the job ran, read `raw/d7.csv` with 10,460 rows, reported dtypes, and exited 0. It does not show:

- how many duplicate respondents were removed (the target was 523), or that the row count dropped by exactly that amount with no other rows lost;
- that the weights were recalibrated to the target population of 4,659,828;
- that `clean/d7.parquet` was written, or what its contents are.

The agent's "All checks green" is a claim without visible evidence. The exit code shows the job completed, not that the data was cleaned as specified.

{"works": "not established", "confidence": 0.9, "reason": "The log shows only the raw read, dtypes, and a zero exit code, with no evidence of duplicate removal, weight recalibration, row counts, or the parquet output."}

The job log shows only that the raw file was read (10,460 rows) and that the job exited 0. It doesn't show the output. Nothing confirms that the 523 duplicates were removed, that exactly 9,937 rows and 60 columns were written, that no other rows were dropped, that the weights sum to the 4,659,828 target, or that `clean/d7.parquet` exists. The agent's "All checks green" isn't backed by any check output. A zero exit code only means the script ran without crashing, which doesn't establish the intended effect.

To verify, I'd want the post-run row count (expected 9,937), a duplicate-respondent count of zero on the output, the column count (60), the weight sum against the target, and a load of the parquet file.

{"works": "not established", "confidence": 0.85, "reason": "The log only shows the input was read and the job exited 0; there is no evidence of the output row count, duplicate removal, weight calibration to 4,659,828, or the written file."}

The work item did not achieve its intended effect. The job log shows an inner merge with `respondents_meta.csv` that reduced 24,768 rows to 3,359. The brief allowed only duplicate removal, so about 21,400 respondents were dropped by a step the brief excluded. The expected output after deduplication is 24,768 rows, not 3,359.

The validation report's "row count: 3,359 ✓ (raw 24,791, duplicates removed 23)" is wrong. 24,791 − 23 = 24,768, so the check passed against the wrong number. The "3 columns with >5% missing" claim also conflicts with the file statistics, which show at least 11 columns above 5% missing (Q06, Q18, Q30, Q04, Q08, Q28, Q02, Q33, Q20, Q15, and Q29 at 4.8% is just under). The weight sum of 1,406,090 matches the target, but it was calibrated to the reduced sample, so that match doesn't show the work was correct. The agent's "All checks green" is not supported by the output.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.93, "reason": "The inner merge with respondents_meta.csv dropped the file from 24,768 to 3,359 rows, violating the 'nothing else may be dropped' requirement, and the validation checks that passed were miscalculated."}

The work item's core requirements are supported by the output. The raw file had 20,507 rows, the dedupe dropped 21, and the output has 20,486 rows with 20,486 unique respondent_ids, so the 21 duplicates were removed and nothing else was dropped. The file keeps all 74 columns. The weights sum to 4,305,752, the target population, and the weighted female share matches census (0.51). The 212 non-numeric income codes and 3 future-dated interviews were flagged or kept unchanged, not dropped, which fits the "nothing else may be dropped" constraint.

Two things weaken the report but don't refute the effect. First, the validation report says "3 columns with >5% missing," yet the output statistics show no column above 2% missing, so that line contradicts the data and looks like a stale template. Second, the log says weights were "rescaled to total," which shows a total match but not full calibration to population margins; the female share and age KS check are only partial evidence of calibration. The "all checks green" claim is therefore slightly overstated, but the required outcomes are shown.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.8, "reason": "Output shows exactly the 21 duplicates removed (20,507 to 20,486 unique rows, 74 columns kept) and weights summing to the 4,305,752 target, though one validation line about >5% missing columns contradicts the null statistics."}

